{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774744,"nonce":"y+xU/dX+1XwB6/8OLyy7toBHNMo=","title":"feat(ui): approve a manual gate from the run detail — one action per gate","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #92 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `feat/ui-approve-gate` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-26T16:21:03Z · Merged: 2026-07-26T17:35:48Z\n\nThere was no way to approve a gate from the UI. Nothing called `/v1/runs/{id}/gates/{step}/approve` — the only references were in the **generated** OpenAPI types (`src/api/schema.ts`).\n\nWhat existed was a dead end: the dashboard inbox lists runs suspended on a gate with an `approve` verb chip and a primary button, but that button is `nav(runHref(r))` — it navigates to a run detail that had **no approve control**. (`RepoView`'s ten `approve` hits are the Environment protection-rule editor — who *may* approve — not approving.)\n\n\nA run can suspend on **several** manual gates at once — a fan-out where two branches each end in their own approval. So this is a *list* of actions, one per gate, not a run-level button that would be ambiguous about what it approved. The inbox row keeps navigating for the same reason: it is per-run, so it cannot name a gate.\n\nAn open gate means one of two quite different things, and conflating them is what makes a gate UI lie: it is your turn, or it is simply not reachable yet. Each row therefore names its unmet needs (`waiting on build`) and disables the button until they succeed, instead of offering a click that would 409.\n\n\nPredicates now live in `src/gates.ts`, shared with `Dag.tsx` which already had its own copy of this test:\n\n- **`waiting` is not a server status.** No `Waiting` variant exists in `StepStatus` and nothing in the engine emits it — it appears only in `Dag.tsx`'s defensive check and in the UI **mock fixtures**, which set the gate to `status: \"waiting\"`. So `just ui-mock` renders a state the server cannot produce. Tolerated and documented in `gates.ts` rather than silently rediscovered per call site.\n- **`ready` IS real**, so a gate can legitimately hold it. Matching only `pending` — my first cut — would have missed it. `Dag.tsx` already knew.\n- **Only `manual` gates are approvable.** `timer` releases itself, `external` needs a signed token (ADR-0034), so a button for either would always fail.\n\n\nTyped `approvals?: string[]` deliberately — the handler emits **two** shapes. The normal path returns `{approvals, released}`, but the late/duplicate-approval branch returns only `{released: true}`, so reading `.approvals.length` unguarded throws there. And `released: false` is a normal outcome (counted, quorum not met), surfaced as `N approvals recorded · awaiting more` rather than looking like nothing happened.\n\n\nAgainst the live colima stack, run `019f9f2b`:\n\n1. parked on its gate → row rendered `approve · waiting on build`, button **disabled**\n2. `build` succeeded → flipped to `approve · waiting for a decision`, button **enabled**\n3. clicked → event log recorded `GateApproved{step:\"approve\", by:\"anonymous\"}` then `GateReleased{step:\"approve\"}`, the run resumed and the bar cleared\n\nPlus 10 unit tests in `gates.test.ts` covering the multi-gate, blocked, non-manual and settled cases. UI suite: **82 passing**.\n\n\nThe 202 declares no response body in the OpenAPI spec (the handler returns an ad-hoc `Json(json!(…))` rather than a `ToSchema` DTO), so the generated type for `data` is `undefined` and the client needs an `as unknown` cast. Fixing that at source would also settle the two-shapes wrinkle above.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}